1.2.8.2 The contextlib module
.............................

The new *note contextlib: 22. module provides some functions and a
decorator that are useful for writing objects for use with the '*note
with: 17.' statement.

  The decorator is called `contextmanager()', and lets you write a
single generator function instead of defining a new class.  The
generator should yield exactly one value.  The code up to the *note
yield: 24. will be executed as the *note __enter__: 18. method, and the
value yielded will be the method's return value that will get bound to
the variable in the '*note with: 17.' statement's *note as: 25. clause,
if any.  The code after the *note yield: 24. will be executed in the
*note __exit__: 19. method.  Any exception raised in the block will be
raised by the *note yield: 24. statement.

  Our database example from the previous section could be written
using this decorator as:

    from contextlib import contextmanager

    @contextmanager
    def db_transaction (connection):
        cursor = connection.cursor()
        try:
            yield cursor
        except:
            connection.rollback()
            raise
        else:
            connection.commit()

    db = DatabaseConnection()
    with db_transaction(db) as cursor:
        ...

The *note contextlib: 22. module also has a `nested(mgr1, mgr2, ...)()'
function that combines a number of context managers so you don't need
to write nested '*note with: 17.' statements.  In this example, the
single '*note with: 17.'  statement both starts a database transaction
and acquires a thread lock:

    lock = threading.Lock()
    with nested (db_transaction(db), lock) as (cursor, locked):
        ...

Finally, the `closing(object)()' function returns _object_ so that it
can be bound to a variable, and calls `object.close' at the end of the
block.

    import urllib, sys
    from contextlib import closing

    with closing(urllib.urlopen('http://www.yahoo.com')) as f:
        for line in f:
            sys.stdout.write(line)


PEP 343 (http://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0343) - The "with" statement
     PEP written by Guido van Rossum and Nick Coghlan; implemented by
     Mike Bland, Guido van Rossum, and Neal Norwitz.  The PEP shows the
     code generated for a '*note with: 17.' statement, which can be
     helpful in learning how the statement works.

  The documentation  for the *note contextlib: 22. module.


