3.1.1.3 Miscellaneous options
.............................

 -- Option: -B
     If given, Python won't try to write `.pyc' or `.pyo' files on the
     import of source modules.  See also *note PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE:
     9e.

     New in version 2.6.

 -- Option: -d
     Turn on parser debugging output (for wizards only, depending on
     compilation options).  See also *note PYTHONDEBUG: 448.

 -- Option: -E
     Ignore all `PYTHON*' environment variables, e.g.  *note
     PYTHONPATH: 391. and *note PYTHONHOME: 44a, that might be set.

     New in version 2.2.

 -- Option: -i
     When a script is passed as first argument or the *note -c: 321.
     option is used, enter interactive mode after executing the script
     or the command, even when *note sys stdin: 443. does not appear to
     be a terminal.  The *note PYTHONSTARTUP: 330. file is not read.

     This can be useful to inspect global variables or a stack trace
     when a script raises an exception.  See also *note PYTHONINSPECT:
     289.

 -- Option: -O
     Turn on basic optimizations.  This changes the filename extension
     for compiled (*note bytecode: 394.) files from `.pyc' to `.pyo'.
     See also *note PYTHONOPTIMIZE: 44b.

 -- Option: -OO
     Discard docstrings in addition to the *note -O: 24f. optimizations.

 -- Option: -Q <arg>
     Division control. The argument must be one of the following:

    `old'
          division of int/int and long/long return an int or long
          (_default_)

    `new'
          new division semantics, i.e. division of int/int and
          long/long returns a float

    `warn'
          old division semantics with a warning for int/int and
          long/long

    `warnall'
          old division semantics with a warning for all uses of the
          division operator

     `Tools/scripts/fixdiv.py'    for a use of `warnall'

     PEP 238 (http://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0238) - Changing the
     division operator

 -- Option: -s
     Don't add user site directory to sys.path

     New in version 2.6.

     PEP 370 (http://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0370) - Per user
     site-packages directory

 -- Option: -S
     Disable the import of the module *note site: 44d. and the
     site-dependent manipulations of *note sys path: 441. that it
     entails.

 -- Option: -t
     Issue a warning when a source file mixes tabs and spaces for
     indentation in a way that makes it depend on the worth of a tab
     expressed in spaces.  Issue an error when the option is given
     twice (`-tt').

 -- Option: -u
     Force stdin, stdout and stderr to be totally unbuffered.  On
     systems where it matters, also put stdin, stdout and stderr in
     binary mode.

     Note that there is internal buffering in *note file readlines:
     44e. and *note File Objects: 44f. (`for line in sys.stdin') which
     is not influenced by this option.  To work around this, you will
     want to use *note file readline: 450. inside a `while 1:' loop.

     See also *note PYTHONUNBUFFERED: 451.

 -- Option: -v
     Print a message each time a module is initialized, showing the
     place (filename or built-in module) from which it is loaded.  When
     given twice (`-vv'), print a message for each file that is checked
     for when searching for a module.  Also provides information on
     module cleanup at exit.  See also *note PYTHONVERBOSE: 452.

 -- Option: -W arg
     Warning control.  Python's warning machinery by default prints
     warning messages to *note sys stderr: 454.  A typical warning
     message has the following form:

         file:line: category: message

     By default, each warning is printed once for each source line
     where it occurs.  This option controls how often warnings are
     printed.

     Multiple *note -W: 453. options may be given; when a warning
     matches more than one option, the action for the last matching
     option is performed.  Invalid *note -W: 453. options are ignored
     (though, a warning message is printed about invalid options when
     the first warning is issued).

     Warnings can also be controlled from within a Python program using
     the *note warnings: 96. module.

     The simplest form of argument is one of the following action
     strings (or a unique abbreviation) by themselves:

    `ignore'
          Ignore all warnings.

    `default'
          Explicitly request the default behavior (printing each
          warning once per source line).

    `all'
          Print a warning each time it occurs (this may generate many
          messages if a warning is triggered repeatedly for the same
          source line, such as inside a loop).

    `module'
          Print each warning only the first time it occurs in each
          module.

    `once'
          Print each warning only the first time it occurs in the
          program.

    `error'
          Raise an exception instead of printing a warning message.

     The full form of argument is:

         action:message:category:module:line

     Here, _action_ is as explained above but only applies to messages
     that match the remaining fields.  Empty fields match all values;
     trailing empty fields may be omitted.  The _message_ field matches
     the start of the warning message printed; this match is
     case-insensitive.  The _category_ field matches the warning
     category.  This must be a class name; the match test whether the
     actual warning category of the message is a subclass of the
     specified warning category.  The full class name must be given.
     The _module_ field matches the (fully-qualified) module name; this
     match is case-sensitive.  The _line_ field matches the line
     number, where zero matches all line numbers and is thus equivalent
     to an omitted line number.

     *note warnings: 96. - the warnings module

     PEP 230 (http://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0230) - Warning
     framework

 -- Option: -x
     Skip the first line of the source, allowing use of non-Unix forms
     of `#!cmd'.  This is intended for a DOS specific hack only.

          Note: The line numbers in error messages will be off by one.

 -- Option: -3
     Warn about Python 3.x incompatibilities which cannot be fixed
     trivially by *note 2to3: 455. Among these are:

        * *note dict has_key: 456.

        * *note apply: 37.

        * *note callable: 457.

        * *note coerce: 458.

        * *note execfile: 22f.

        * *note reduce: d.

        * *note reload: 38c.

     Using these will emit a *note DeprecationWarning: 226.

     New in version 2.6.


