5.4 Built-in Constants
======================

A small number of constants live in the built-in namespace.  They are:

 -- Data: False
     The false value of the *note bool: 236. type.

     New in version 2.3.

 -- Data: True
     The true value of the *note bool: 236. type.

     New in version 2.3.

 -- Data: None
     The sole value of *note types NoneType: 641.  `None' is frequently
     used to represent the absence of a value, as when default
     arguments are not passed to a function.

     Changed in version 2.4: Assignments to `None' are illegal and
     raise a *note SyntaxError: 2ac.

 -- Data: NotImplemented
     Special value which can be returned by the "rich comparison"
     special methods (*note __eq__: 8b, *note __lt__: 2d2, and
     friends), to indicate that the comparison is not implemented with
     respect to the other type.

 -- Data: Ellipsis
     Special value used in conjunction with extended slicing syntax.

 -- Data: __debug__
     This constant is true if Python was not started with an *note -O:
     24f. option.  It cannot be reassigned.  See also the *note assert:
     24e. statement.

     Note: The name *note None: 174. cannot be reassigned (assignments
     to it, even as an attribute name, raise *note SyntaxError: 2ac.),
     so it can be considered a "true" constant.

