5.5.11 Other Built-in Types
---------------------------

The interpreter supports several other kinds of objects. Most of these
support only one or two operations.

* Menu:

* Modules: Modules<2>.
* Classes and Class Instances::
* Functions::
* Methods::
* Code Objects::
* Type Objects::
* The Null Object::
* The Ellipsis Object::
* Boolean Values::
* Internal Objects::


File: python.info,  Node: Modules<2>,  Next: Classes and Class Instances,  Up: Other Built-in Types

5.5.11.1 Modules
................

The only special operation on a module is attribute access: `m.name',
where _m_ is a module and _name_ accesses a name defined in _m_'s
symbol table.  Module attributes can be assigned to.  (Note that the
*note import: 16a.  statement is not, strictly speaking, an operation
on a module object; `import foo' does not require a module object named
_foo_ to exist, rather it requires an (external) _definition_ for a
module named _foo_ somewhere.)

  A special member of every module is `__dict__'. This is the dictionary
containing the module's symbol table. Modifying this dictionary will
actually change the module's symbol table, but direct assignment to the
`__dict__' attribute is not possible (you can write `m.__dict__['a'] =
1', which defines `m.a' to be `1', but you can't write `m.__dict__ =
{}').  Modifying `__dict__' directly is not recommended.

  Modules built into the interpreter are written like this: `<module
'sys' (built-in)>'.  If loaded from a file, they are written as
`<module 'os' from '/usr/local/lib/pythonX.Y/os.pyc'>'.


