5.7.1.2 String Formatting
.........................

New in version 2.6.

  The built-in str and unicode classes provide the ability to do
complex variable substitutions and value formatting via the *note str
format: 3e. method described in PEP 3101
(http://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-3101).  The *note Formatter: 70d.
class in the *note string: 169. module allows you to create and
customize your own string formatting behaviors using the same
implementation as the built-in *note format: 3d. method.

 -- Class: string.Formatter
     The *note Formatter: 70d. class has the following public methods:

      -- Method: format (format_string, *args, *kwargs)
          *note format: 3d. is the primary API method.  It takes a
          format template string, and an arbitrary set of positional
          and keyword argument.  *note format: 3d. is just a wrapper
          that calls *note vformat: 70f.

      -- Method: vformat (format_string, args, kwargs)
          This function does the actual work of formatting.  It is
          exposed as a separate function for cases where you want to
          pass in a predefined dictionary of arguments, rather than
          unpacking and repacking the dictionary as individual
          arguments using the `*args' and `**kwds' syntax.  *note
          vformat: 70f. does the work of breaking up the format template
          string into character data and replacement fields.  It calls
          the various methods described below.

     In addition, the *note Formatter: 70d. defines a number of methods
     that are intended to be replaced by subclasses:

      -- Method: parse (format_string)
          Loop over the format_string and return an iterable of tuples
          (_literal_text_, _field_name_, _format_spec_, _conversion_).
          This is used by *note vformat: 70f. to break the string in to
          either literal text, or replacement fields.

          The values in the tuple conceptually represent a span of
          literal text followed by a single replacement field.  If
          there is no literal text (which can happen if two replacement
          fields occur consecutively), then _literal_text_ will be a
          zero-length string.  If there is no replacement field, then
          the values of _field_name_, _format_spec_ and _conversion_
          will be `None'.

      -- Method: get_field (field_name, args, kwargs)
          Given _field_name_ as returned by *note parse: 710. (see
          above), convert it to an object to be formatted.  Returns a
          tuple (obj, used_key).  The default version takes strings of
          the form defined in PEP 3101
          (http://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-3101), such as "0[name]"
          or "label.title".  _args_ and _kwargs_ are as passed in to
          *note vformat: 70f.  The return value _used_key_ has the same
          meaning as the _key_ parameter to *note get_value: 712.

      -- Method: get_value (key, args, kwargs)
          Retrieve a given field value.  The _key_ argument will be
          either an integer or a string.  If it is an integer, it
          represents the index of the positional argument in _args_; if
          it is a string, then it represents a named argument in
          _kwargs_.

          The _args_ parameter is set to the list of positional
          arguments to *note vformat: 70f, and the _kwargs_ parameter
          is set to the dictionary of keyword arguments.

          For compound field names, these functions are only called for
          the first component of the field name; Subsequent components
          are handled through normal attribute and indexing operations.

          So for example, the field expression '0.name' would cause
          *note get_value: 712. to be called with a _key_ argument of
          0.  The `name' attribute will be looked up after *note
          get_value: 712. returns by calling the built-in *note
          getattr: 62f. function.

          If the index or keyword refers to an item that does not
          exist, then an *note IndexError: 2f6. or *note KeyError: 1e1.
          should be raised.

      -- Method: check_unused_args (used_args, args, kwargs)
          Implement checking for unused arguments if desired.  The
          arguments to this function is the set of all argument keys
          that were actually referred to in the format string (integers
          for positional arguments, and strings for named arguments),
          and a reference to the _args_ and _kwargs_ that was passed to
          vformat.  The set of unused args can be calculated from these
          parameters.  *note check_unused_args: 713. is assumed to
          throw an exception if the check fails.

      -- Method: format_field (value, format_spec)
          *note format_field: 714. simply calls the global *note
          format: 3d. built-in.  The method is provided so that
          subclasses can override it.

      -- Method: convert_field (value, conversion)
          Converts the value (returned by *note get_field: 711.) given
          a conversion type (as in the tuple returned by the *note
          parse: 710. method).  The default version understands 'r'
          (repr) and 's' (str) conversion types.


