5.8.7 `sets' -- Unordered collections of unique elements
--------------------------------------------------------

New in version 2.3.

  Deprecated since version 2.6: The built-in `set'/`frozenset' types
replace this module.

  The *note sets: 11a. module provides classes for constructing and
manipulating unordered collections of unique elements.  Common uses
include membership testing, removing duplicates from a sequence, and
computing standard math operations on sets such as intersection, union,
difference, and symmetric difference.

  Like other collections, sets support `x in set', `len(set)', and `for
x in set'.  Being an unordered collection, sets do not record element
position or order of insertion.  Accordingly, sets do not support
indexing, slicing, or other sequence-like behavior.

  Most set applications use the *note Set: 927. class which provides
every set method except for *note __hash__: 89. For advanced
applications requiring a hash method, the *note ImmutableSet: 928.
class adds a *note __hash__: 89. method but omits methods which alter
the contents of the set. Both *note Set: 927. and *note ImmutableSet:
928.  derive from `BaseSet', an abstract class useful for determining
whether something is a set: `isinstance(obj, BaseSet)'.

  The set classes are implemented using dictionaries.  Accordingly, the
requirements for set elements are the same as those for dictionary
keys; namely, that the element defines both *note __eq__: 8b. and *note
__hash__: 89. As a result, sets cannot contain mutable elements such as
lists or dictionaries. However, they can contain immutable collections
such as tuples or instances of *note ImmutableSet: 928.  For
convenience in implementing sets of sets, inner sets are automatically
converted to immutable form, for example, `Set([Set(['dog'])])' is
transformed to `Set([ImmutableSet(['dog'])])'.

 -- Class: sets.Set ([iterable])
     Constructs a new empty *note Set: 927. object.  If the optional
     _iterable_ parameter is supplied, updates the set with elements
     obtained from iteration.  All of the elements in _iterable_ should
     be immutable or be transformable to an immutable using the
     protocol described in section *note Protocol for automatic
     conversion to immutable: 929.

 -- Class: sets.ImmutableSet ([iterable])
     Constructs a new empty *note ImmutableSet: 928. object.  If the
     optional _iterable_ parameter is supplied, updates the set with
     elements obtained from iteration.  All of the elements in
     _iterable_ should be immutable or be transformable to an immutable
     using the protocol described in section *note Protocol for
     automatic conversion to immutable: 929.

     Because *note ImmutableSet: 928. objects provide a *note __hash__:
     89. method, they can be used as set elements or as dictionary
     keys.  *note ImmutableSet: 928.  objects do not have methods for
     adding or removing elements, so all of the elements must be known
     when the constructor is called.

