5.9.4.9 Working with threads
............................

The *note getcontext: a21. function accesses a different *note Context:
a5f. object for each thread.  Having separate thread contexts means
that threads may make changes (such as `getcontext.prec=10') without
interfering with other threads.

  Likewise, the *note setcontext: a22. function automatically assigns
its target to the current thread.

  If *note setcontext: a22. has not been called before *note
getcontext: a21, then *note getcontext: a21. will automatically create
a new context for use in the current thread.

  The new context is copied from a prototype context called
_DefaultContext_. To control the defaults so that each thread will use
the same values throughout the application, directly modify the
_DefaultContext_ object. This should be done _before_ any threads are
started so that there won't be a race condition between threads calling
*note getcontext: a21. For example:

    # Set applicationwide defaults for all threads about to be launched
    DefaultContext.prec = 12
    DefaultContext.rounding = ROUND_DOWN
    DefaultContext.traps = ExtendedContext.traps.copy()
    DefaultContext.traps[InvalidOperation] = 1
    setcontext(DefaultContext)

    # Afterwards, the threads can be started
    t1.start()
    t2.start()
    t3.start()
     . . .



