5.9.7 `itertools' -- Functions creating iterators for efficient looping
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

New in version 2.3.

  This module implements a number of *note iterator: 62a. building
blocks inspired by constructs from APL, Haskell, and SML.  Each has
been recast in a form suitable for Python.

  The module standardizes a core set of fast, memory efficient tools
that are useful by themselves or in combination.  Together, they form
an "iterator algebra" making it possible to construct specialized tools
succinctly and efficiently in pure Python.

  For instance, SML provides a tabulation tool: `tabulate(f)' which
produces a sequence `f(0), f(1), ...'.  The same effect can be achieved
in Python by combining *note imap: ad2. and *note count: ad3. to form
`imap(f, count())'.

  These tools and their built-in counterparts also work well with the
high-speed functions in the *note operator: c2. module.  For example,
the multiplication operator can be mapped across two vectors to form an
efficient dot-product: `sum(imap(operator.mul, vector1, vector2))'.

  *Infinite Iterators:*

Iterator               Arguments             Results                                               Example
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- 
*note count: ad3.      start                 start, start+1, start+2, ...                          `count(10) --> 10 11 12 13 14 ...'
*note cycle: ad4.      p                     p0, p1, ... plast, p0, p1, ...                        `cycle('ABCD') --> A B C D A B C D ...'
*note repeat: 8ed.     elem [,n]             elem, elem, elem, ... endlessly or up to n times      `repeat(10, 3) --> 10 10 10'

  *Iterators terminating on the shortest input sequence:*

Iterator                 Arguments                        Results                                               Example
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- 
*note chain: ad5.        p, q, ...                        p0, p1, ... plast, q0, q1, ...                        `chain('ABC', 'DEF') --> A B C D E F'
*note dropwhile: ad6.    pred, seq                        seq[n], seq[n+1], starting when pred fails            `dropwhile(lambda x: x<5, [1,4,6,4,1]) --> 6 4 1'
*note groupby: ad7.      iterable[, keyfunc]              sub-iterators grouped by value of keyfunc(v)          
*note ifilter: 62b.      pred, seq                        elements of seq where pred(elem) is True              `ifilter(lambda x: x%2, range(10)) --> 1 3 5 7 9'
*note ifilterfalse: 62c. pred, seq                        elements of seq where pred(elem) is False             `ifilterfalse(lambda x: x%2, range(10)) --> 0 2 4 6 8'
*note islice: 1a4.       seq, [start,] stop [, step]      elements from seq[start:stop:step]                    `islice('ABCDEFG', 2, None) --> C D E F G'
*note imap: ad2.         func, p, q, ...                  func(p0, q0), func(p1, q1), ...                       `imap(pow, (2,3,10), (5,2,3)) --> 32 9 1000'
*note starmap: ad8.      func, seq                        func(*seq[0]), func(*seq[1]), ...                     `starmap(pow, [(2,5), (3,2), (10,3)]) --> 32 9 1000'
*note tee: ad9.          it, n                            it1, it2 , ... itn  splits one iterator into n        
*note takewhile: ada.    pred, seq                        seq[0], seq[1], until pred fails                      `takewhile(lambda x: x<5, [1,4,6,4,1]) --> 1 4'
*note izip: 1f4.         p, q, ...                        (p[0], q[0]), (p[1], q[1]), ...                       `izip('ABCD', 'xy') --> Ax By'
*note izip_longest: adb. p, q, ...                        (p[0], q[0]), (p[1], q[1]), ...                       `izip_longest('ABCD', 'xy', fillvalue='-') --> Ax By C- D-'

  *Combinatoric generators:*

Iterator                                           Arguments                Results
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- 
*note product: adc.                                p, q, ... [repeat=1]     cartesian product, equivalent to a nested for-loop
*note permutations: add.                           p[, r]                   r-length tuples, all possible orderings, no repeated elements
*note combinations: ade.                           p, r                     r-length tuples, in sorted order, no repeated elements
                                                                             
`product('ABCD', repeat=2)'                                                 `AA AB AC AD BA BB BC BD CA CB CC CD DA DB DC DD'
`permutations('ABCD', 2)'                                                   `AB AC AD BA BC BD CA CB CD DA DB DC'
`combinations('ABCD', 2)'                                                   `AB AC AD BC BD CD'

