5.11.1.6 Pickling and unpickling normal class instances
.......................................................

 -- Method: object.__getinitargs__ ()
     When a pickled class instance is unpickled, its *note __init__:
     150. method is normally _not_ invoked.  If it is desirable that
     the *note __init__: 150. method be called on unpickling, an
     old-style class can define a method *note __getinitargs__: c38,
     which should return a _tuple_ containing the arguments to be
     passed to the class constructor (*note __init__: 150. for
     example).  The *note __getinitargs__: c38. method is called at
     pickle time; the tuple it returns is incorporated in the pickle
     for the instance.

 -- Method: object.__getnewargs__ ()
     New-style types can provide a *note __getnewargs__: 248. method
     that is used for protocol 2.  Implementing this method is needed
     if the type establishes some internal invariants when the instance
     is created, or if the memory allocation is affected by the values
     passed to the *note __new__: 50f. method for the type (as it is
     for tuples and strings).  Instances of a *note new-style class:
     3d9.  `C' are created using

         obj = C.__new__(C, *args)

     where _args_ is the result of calling *note __getnewargs__: 248.
     on the original object; if there is no *note __getnewargs__: 248,
     an empty tuple is assumed.

 -- Method: object.__getstate__ ()
     Classes can further influence how their instances are pickled; if
     the class defines the method *note __getstate__: 246, it is called
     and the return state is pickled as the contents for the instance,
     instead of the contents of the instance's dictionary.  If there is
     no *note __getstate__: 246. method, the instance's *note __dict__:
     6dc. is pickled.

 -- Method: object.__setstate__ ()
     Upon unpickling, if the class also defines the method *note
     __setstate__: 247, it is called with the unpickled state. (5) If
     there is no *note __setstate__: 247. method, the pickled state
     must be a dictionary and its items are assigned to the new
     instance's dictionary.  If a class defines both *note
     __getstate__: 246. and *note __setstate__: 247, the state object
     needn't be a dictionary and these methods can do what they want.
     (6)

          Note: For *note new-style class: 3d9.es, if *note
          __getstate__: 246. returns a false value, the *note
          __setstate__: 247. method will not be called.

     Note: At unpickling time, some methods like *note __getattr__: 81,
     *note __getattribute__: 92, or *note __setattr__: 29e. may be
     called upon the instance.  In case those methods rely on some
     internal invariant being true, the type should implement either
     *note __getinitargs__: c38. or *note __getnewargs__: 248. to
     establish such an invariant; otherwise, neither *note __new__:
     50f. nor *note __init__: 150. will be called.


