5.11.13.6 Introduction
......................

SQLite natively supports the following types: `NULL', `INTEGER',
`REAL', `TEXT', `BLOB'.

  The following Python types can thus be sent to SQLite without any
problem:

Python type                       SQLite type
---------------------------------------------------- 
*note None: 174.                  `NULL'
*note int: 6e.                    `INTEGER'
*note long: 6f.                   `INTEGER'
*note float: 85.                  `REAL'
*note str: 4c. (UTF8-encoded)     `TEXT'
*note unicode: 271.               `TEXT'
*note buffer: 55.                 `BLOB'

  This is how SQLite types are converted to Python types by default:

SQLite type       Python type
--------------------------------------------------------------------- 
`NULL'            *note None: 174.
`INTEGER'         *note int: 6e. or *note long: 6f, depending on
                  size
`REAL'            *note float: 85.
`TEXT'            depends on *note text_factory: cb4, *note
                  unicode: 271. by default
`BLOB'            *note buffer: 55.

  The type system of the *note sqlite3: db. module is extensible in two
ways: you can store additional Python types in a SQLite database via
object adaptation, and you can let the *note sqlite3: db. module
convert SQLite types to different Python types via converters.


