5.15.2 `io' -- Core tools for working with streams
--------------------------------------------------

New in version 2.6.

  The *note io: 54. module provides the Python interfaces to stream
handling.  The built-in *note open: 1b. function is defined in this
module.

  At the top of the I/O hierarchy is the abstract base class *note
IOBase: eff.  It defines the basic interface to a stream.  Note,
however, that there is no separation between reading and writing to
streams; implementations are allowed to throw an *note IOError: 158. if
they do not support a given operation.

  Extending *note IOBase: eff. is *note RawIOBase: f00. which deals
simply with the reading and writing of raw bytes to a stream.  *note
FileIO: f01. subclasses *note RawIOBase: f00. to provide an interface
to files in the machine's file system.

  *note BufferedIOBase: f02. deals with buffering on a raw byte stream
(*note RawIOBase: f00.).  Its subclasses, *note BufferedWriter: f03,
*note BufferedReader: f04, and *note BufferedRWPair: f05. buffer
streams that are readable, writable, and both readable and writable.
*note BufferedRandom: f06. provides a buffered interface to random
access streams.  *note BytesIO: f07. is a simple stream of in-memory
bytes.

  Another *note IOBase: eff. subclass, *note TextIOBase: f08, deals with
streams whose bytes represent text, and handles encoding and decoding
from and to strings. *note TextIOWrapper: f09, which extends it, is a
buffered text interface to a buffered raw stream (*note BufferedIOBase:
f02.). Finally, *note StringIO: 56. is an in-memory stream for text.

  Argument names are not part of the specification, and only the
arguments of *note open: f0a. are intended to be used as keyword
arguments.

