5.20.22 `Cookie' -- HTTP state management
-----------------------------------------

     Note: The *note Cookie: a9. module has been renamed to
     `http.cookies' in Python 3.0.  The *note 2to3: 945. tool will
     automatically adapt imports when converting your sources to 3.0.

The *note Cookie: a9. module defines classes for abstracting the
concept of cookies, an HTTP state management mechanism. It supports
both simple string-only cookies, and provides an abstraction for having
any serializable data-type as cookie value.

  The module formerly strictly applied the parsing rules described in
the RFC 2109 (http://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc2109.html) and RFC 2068
(http://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc2068.html) specifications.  It has since
been discovered that MSIE 3.0x doesn't follow the character rules
outlined in those specs.  As a result, the parsing rules used are a bit
less strict.

     Note: On encountering an invalid cookie, *note CookieError: 1b8c.
     is raised, so if your cookie data comes from a browser you should
     always prepare for invalid data and catch *note CookieError: 1b8c.
     on parsing.

 -- Exception: Cookie.CookieError
     Exception failing because of RFC 2109
     (http://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc2109.html) invalidity: incorrect
     attributes, incorrect `Set-Cookie' header, etc.

 -- Class: Cookie.BaseCookie ([input])
     This class is a dictionary-like object whose keys are strings and
     whose values are *note Morsel: 1b8e. instances. Note that upon
     setting a key to a value, the value is first converted to a *note
     Morsel: 1b8e. containing the key and the value.

     If _input_ is given, it is passed to the *note load: 1b8f. method.

 -- Class: Cookie.SimpleCookie ([input])
     This class derives from *note BaseCookie: 1b8d. and overrides
     `value_decode()' and `value_encode()' to be the identity and *note
     str: 4c. respectively.

 -- Class: Cookie.SerialCookie ([input])
     This class derives from *note BaseCookie: 1b8d. and overrides
     `value_decode()' and `value_encode()' to be the *note pickle
     loads: c2a. and *note pickle dumps: 244.

     Deprecated since version 2.3: Reading pickled values from
     untrusted cookie data is a huge security hole, as pickle strings
     can be crafted to cause arbitrary code to execute on your server.
     It is supported for backwards compatibility only, and may
     eventually go away.

 -- Class: Cookie.SmartCookie ([input])
     This class derives from *note BaseCookie: 1b8d. It overrides
     `value_decode()' to be *note pickle loads: c2a. if it is a valid
     pickle, and otherwise the value itself. It overrides
     `value_encode()' to be *note pickle dumps: 244. unless it is a
     string, in which case it returns the value itself.

     Deprecated since version 2.3: The same security warning from *note
     SerialCookie: 1b91. applies here.

  A further security note is warranted.  For backwards compatibility,
the *note Cookie: a9. module exports a class named *note Cookie: a9.
which is just an alias for *note SmartCookie: 1b92.  This is probably a
mistake and will likely be removed in a future version.  You should not
use the *note Cookie: a9. class in your applications, for the same
reason why you should not use the *note SerialCookie: 1b91. class.

Module *note cookielib: 209.
     HTTP cookie handling for web _clients_.  The *note cookielib: 209.
     and *note Cookie: a9.  modules do not depend on each other.

RFC 2109 (http://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc2109.html) - HTTP State Management Mechanism
     This is the state management specification implemented by this
     module.

