5.25.3.3 Re-using old test code
...............................

Some users will find that they have existing test code that they would
like to run from *note unittest: 402, without converting every old test
function to a *note TestCase: 1eaa. subclass.

  For this reason, *note unittest: 402. provides a *note
FunctionTestCase: 1ee8. class.  This subclass of *note TestCase: 1eaa.
can be used to wrap an existing test function.  Set-up and tear-down
functions can also be provided.

  Given the following test function:

    def testSomething():
        something = makeSomething()
        assert something.name is not None
        # ...

one can create an equivalent test case instance as follows:

    testcase = unittest.FunctionTestCase(testSomething)

If there are additional set-up and tear-down methods that should be
called as part of the test case's operation, they can also be provided
like so:

    testcase = unittest.FunctionTestCase(testSomething,
                                         setUp=makeSomethingDB,
                                         tearDown=deleteSomethingDB)

To make migrating existing test suites easier, *note unittest: 402.
supports tests raising *note AssertionError: 5f1. to indicate test
failure. However, it is recommended that you use the explicit
`TestCase.fail*()' and `TestCase.assert*()' methods instead, as future
versions of *note unittest: 402.  may treat *note AssertionError: 5f1.
differently.

     Note: Even though *note FunctionTestCase: 1ee8. can be used to
     quickly convert an existing test base over to a *note unittest:
     402.-based system, this approach is not recommended.  Taking the
     time to set up proper *note TestCase: 1eaa. subclasses will make
     future test refactorings infinitely easier.


