7.7.5.14 Set Objects
....................

New in version 2.5.

  This section details the public API for *note set: 11b. and *note
frozenset: 11c.  objects.  Any functionality not listed below is best
accessed using the either the abstract object protocol (including *note
PyObject_CallMethod: 2741, *note PyObject_RichCompareBool: 2737, *note
PyObject_Hash: 2743, *note PyObject_Repr: 2738, *note PyObject_IsTrue:
2744, *note PyObject_Print: 272c, and *note PyObject_GetIter: 274c.) or
the abstract number protocol (including *note PyNumber_And: 2760, *note
PyNumber_Subtract: 2752, *note PyNumber_Or: 2762, *note PyNumber_Xor:
2761, *note PyNumber_InPlaceAnd: 276d, *note PyNumber_InPlaceSubtract:
2764, *note PyNumber_InPlaceOr: 276f, and *note PyNumber_InPlaceXor:
276e.).

 -- type: PySetObject
     This subtype of *note PyObject: 17e. is used to hold the internal
     data for both *note set: 11b. and *note frozenset: 11c. objects.
     It is like a *note PyDictObject: 28e9.  in that it is a fixed size
     for small sets (much like tuple storage) and will point to a
     separate, variable sized block of memory for medium and large sized
     sets (much like list storage). None of the fields of this
     structure should be considered public and are subject to change.
     All access should be done through the documented API rather than
     by manipulating the values in the structure.

 -- var: PyTypeObject PySet_Type
     This is an instance of *note PyTypeObject: 2636. representing the
     Python *note set: 11b. type.

 -- var: PyTypeObject PyFrozenSet_Type
     This is an instance of *note PyTypeObject: 2636. representing the
     Python *note frozenset: 11c. type.

  The following type check macros work on pointers to any Python
object. Likewise, the constructor functions work with any iterable
Python object.

 -- Function: int PySet_Check (PyObject *p)
     Return true if _p_ is a *note set: 11b. object or an instance of a
     subtype.

     New in version 2.6.

 -- Function: int PyFrozenSet_Check (PyObject *p)
     Return true if _p_ is a *note frozenset: 11c. object or an
     instance of a subtype.

     New in version 2.6.

 -- Function: int PyAnySet_Check (PyObject *p)
     Return true if _p_ is a *note set: 11b. object, a *note frozenset:
     11c. object, or an instance of a subtype.

 -- Function: int PyAnySet_CheckExact (PyObject *p)
     Return true if _p_ is a *note set: 11b. object or a *note
     frozenset: 11c. object but not an instance of a subtype.

 -- Function: int PyFrozenSet_CheckExact (PyObject *p)
     Return true if _p_ is a *note frozenset: 11c. object but not an
     instance of a subtype.

 -- Function: PyObject* PySet_New (PyObject *iterable)
     Return value: New reference.

     Return a new *note set: 11b. containing objects returned by the
     _iterable_.  The _iterable_ may be _NULL_ to create a new empty
     set.  Return the new set on success or _NULL_ on failure.  Raise
     *note TypeError: 47. if _iterable_ is not actually iterable.  The
     constructor is also useful for copying a set (`c=set(s)').

 -- Function: PyObject* PyFrozenSet_New (PyObject *iterable)
     Return value: New reference.

     Return a new *note frozenset: 11c. containing objects returned by
     the _iterable_.  The _iterable_ may be _NULL_ to create a new
     empty frozenset.  Return the new set on success or _NULL_ on
     failure.  Raise *note TypeError: 47. if _iterable_ is not actually
     iterable.

     Changed in version 2.6: Now guaranteed to return a brand-new *note
     frozenset: 11c.  Formerly, frozensets of zero-length were a
     singleton.  This got in the way of building-up new frozensets with
     `PySet_Add()'.

  The following functions and macros are available for instances of
*note set: 11b.  or *note frozenset: 11c. or instances of their
subtypes.

 -- Function: Py_ssize_t PySet_Size (PyObject *anyset)
     Return the length of a *note set: 11b. or *note frozenset: 11c.
     object. Equivalent to `len(anyset)'.  Raises a `PyExc_SystemError'
     if _anyset_ is not a *note set: 11b, *note frozenset: 11c, or an
     instance of a subtype.

     Changed in version 2.5: This function returned an `int'. This
     might require changes in your code for properly supporting 64-bit
     systems.

 -- Function: Py_ssize_t PySet_GET_SIZE (PyObject *anyset)
     Macro form of *note PySet_Size: 1d2. without error checking.

 -- Function: int PySet_Contains (PyObject *anyset, PyObject *key)
     Return 1 if found, 0 if not found, and -1 if an error is
     encountered.  Unlike the Python *note __contains__: 69. method,
     this function does not automatically convert unhashable sets into
     temporary frozensets.  Raise a *note TypeError: 47. if the _key_
     is unhashable. Raise `PyExc_SystemError' if _anyset_ is not a
     *note set: 11b, *note frozenset: 11c, or an instance of a subtype.

 -- Function: int PySet_Add (PyObject *set, PyObject *key)
     Add _key_ to a *note set: 11b. instance.  Does not apply to *note
     frozenset: 11c.  instances.  Return 0 on success or -1 on failure.
     Raise a *note TypeError: 47. if the _key_ is unhashable. Raise a
     *note MemoryError: 6ed. if there is no room to grow.  Raise a
     *note SystemError: 6f0. if _set_ is an not an instance of *note
     set: 11b. or its subtype.

     Changed in version 2.6: Now works with instances of *note
     frozenset: 11c. or its subtypes.  Like *note PyTuple_SetItem:
     2623. in that it can be used to fill-in the values of brand new
     frozensets before they are exposed to other code.

  The following functions are available for instances of *note set:
11b. or its subtypes but not for instances of *note frozenset: 11c. or
its subtypes.

 -- Function: int PySet_Discard (PyObject *set, PyObject *key)
     Return 1 if found and removed, 0 if not found (no action taken),
     and -1 if an error is encountered.  Does not raise *note KeyError:
     1e1. for missing keys.  Raise a *note TypeError: 47. if the _key_
     is unhashable.  Unlike the Python `discard()' method, this
     function does not automatically convert unhashable sets into
     temporary frozensets. Raise `PyExc_SystemError' if _set_ is an not
     an instance of *note set: 11b. or its subtype.

 -- Function: PyObject* PySet_Pop (PyObject *set)
     Return value: New reference.

     Return a new reference to an arbitrary object in the _set_, and
     removes the object from the _set_.  Return _NULL_ on failure.
     Raise *note KeyError: 1e1. if the set is empty. Raise a *note
     SystemError: 6f0. if _set_ is an not an instance of *note set:
     11b. or its subtype.

 -- Function: int PySet_Clear (PyObject *set)
     Empty an existing set of all elements.


