12.4.4.1 How do I delete a file? (And other file questions...)
..............................................................

Use `os.remove(filename)' or `os.unlink(filename)'; for documentation,
see the *note os: c3. module.  The two functions are identical;
`unlink()' is simply the name of the Unix system call for this function.

  To remove a directory, use *note os rmdir: c14.; use *note os mkdir:
ea4. to create one.  `os.makedirs(path)' will create any intermediate
directories in `path' that don't exist. `os.removedirs(path)' will
remove intermediate directories as long as they're empty; if you want
to delete an entire directory tree and its contents, use *note shutil
rmtree: c12.

  To rename a file, use `os.rename(old_path, new_path)'.

  To truncate a file, open it using `f = open(filename, "r+")', and use
`f.truncate(offset)'; offset defaults to the current seek position.
There's also ``os.ftruncate(fd, offset)' for files opened with *note os
open: 3ec, where `fd' is the file descriptor (a small integer).

  The *note shutil: d3. module also contains a number of functions to
work on files including *note copyfile: c0c, *note copytree: d2, and
*note rmtree: c12.


